ZERO WEB CSS PROFILE 0.15

This original Zero memory-only worker implements a bounded experimental CSS
cascade. It does not contain Chromium code and is not a complete CSS engine.
Stylesheet text is external interoperability input, never a project language.

Supported behavior

Selectors: a single ASCII tag, .class, #id or *. Tag names are ASCII-insensitive;
id/class values are case-sensitive. Class attributes contain whitespace-delimited
tokens. Specificity is0/1/10/100; later declarations/rules win equal specificity.
Inline declarations override stylesheet rules. Color and font size inherit from
the computed parent. The declaration's own invalid value does not erase an
earlier valid value. Whitespace/comments and quoted declaration values are parsed
with bounded scanning; braces inside strings do not terminate rules.

Properties: color, background-color, font-size, display, padding, margin, width.
Colors accept #rgb, #rrggbb and black/white/red/green/blue/gray/grey/yellow/cyan/
magenta/orange/purple/navy/teal/silver/maroon/olive/rebeccapurple. Background also
accepts transparent, represented by unsigned -1. Background is not inherited.
Font size is an integer1..96px. Padding, margin and width accept one nonnegative
integer0..4096px; unitless zero is allowed. Display accepts none, block or inline.
Default color is0x1f2937; default font size16px; default background transparent.
Block tags have a basic block default. Head/title/meta/link/style/script default
to hidden; style/script content cannot be rendered by an author display rule.
Ancestor display:none hides every descendant.

Limits and unsupported syntax

Maximum512 nodes,65536 input bytes and128 accepted stylesheet rules. Unsupported
selectors (including combinations, descendants, comma lists and pseudoclasses),
properties, values, at-rules, functions and !important are ignored and set warning
bit2. No resource is fetched. Unsupported at-rule blocks are skipped as balanced
units; nested rules are not applied outside their unsupported condition.
This profile lacks the full CSS tokenizer, escaping, cascade origins/layers,
important handling, style-sheet loading, inherited keywords, custom properties,
flex/grid/table layout, relative units, shaping and media query evaluation.
Unclosed stylesheet rules are ignored rather than HTML/CSS-standard recovery.
The separate renderer determines how this bounded computed style is painted.

ABI and composition

declarations.zero.part documents the shared arena. css.zero.part uses only@web
and@v0..@v31. Its fork context isweb+1280; stack isweb+1146880,81920bytes.
After fork, prime once by switch[web+1024|web+1280]. Then each identical switch
processes the current descriptor. The caller owns valid disjoint memory and
must not expose computed styles on nonzero error. Existing errors are preserved.
Error28 is rule capacity;70 is invalid descriptor/parent/source bounds.
The caller must supply DOM fields as described by the common contract.

Verification

css-check.zero consists of common declarations, css-check-head.zero.part,
css.zero.part and css-check-tail.zero.part, in that order. The original Zero
checker has51 semantic/boundary cases and100 deterministic malformed CSS probes.
Checks cover specificity, source order, inherited values, inline precedence,
comments/quotes, at-rule isolation, unsupported syntax, transparent backgrounds,
limits128/129, invalid parent/source pointers, guards and immutable input.
All151 pass on native Linux x64 and Windows x64 under Wine. Native Windows and
physical hardware were not tested. Compile-only smoke succeeds for raw/UEFI32/64
with a caller-provided arena. No guest execution is claimed. Existing compiler
app32/app64 modes reject fork/switch, so these worker forms cannot yet be shipped
as standalone guest applications without further runtime/compiler integration.
